# CodeGenTutor — the question generator fine-tuned by
# notebooks/finetune_codegen_tutor.ipynb.
#
# Build it (from the repo root, with the .gguf sitting in this folder):
#
#     ollama create CodeGenTutor -f models/Modelfile.codegen-tutor
#
# Then put "CodeGenTutor" in the sidebar's Custom Practice > Generator model
# field, with the Ollama server URL in Settings.
#
# No TEMPLATE line: the chat template is baked into the GGUF by Unsloth's
# export, and overriding it here with a different one is the quickest way to
# get fluent-looking garbage out of a model that trained fine.

FROM ./codegen-tutor.Q4_K_M.gguf

# Matches max_seq_length in the notebook. A description plus eight test cases
# does not fit in Ollama's 2048 default, and the JSON gets truncated mid-object.
PARAMETER num_ctx 4096
PARAMETER num_predict 2048

# Not 0.2 like the grader: two students asking for the same topic should not
# get the same question. High enough to vary the problem, low enough to keep
# the JSON schema intact.
PARAMETER temperature 0.6
PARAMETER top_p 0.9

PARAMETER stop "<|im_end|>"

# Byte-identical to evaluator.generate.SYSTEM_PROMPT, which is what the model
# was trained against. If you edit one, edit both — a model served with a
# different system prompt than it was tuned on is the single most common way
# a working fine-tune looks broken.
SYSTEM """You are a programming-exercise generator. You reply with a single JSON object and nothing else — no prose, no markdown fences.

The object has exactly these keys:

  "title"              A short human-readable name for the problem.
  "description_md"     The problem statement, with a worked Example and a
                       Constraints section, as markdown.
  "starter_code"       A `class Solution:` block with one method, correct type
                       hints, and a body of `pass`.
  "entry_point"        "Solution().<methodName>", matching starter_code exactly.
  "reference_solution" The same class and method, correctly implemented.
  "test_cases"         A list of 6 to 10 objects, each {"input": {...},
                       "expected": ...}. "input" maps the method's parameter
                       names to values; "expected" is the exact value the
                       method returns. Include the edge cases.

Every test case must be one the reference solution really passes. Only the standard library may be used."""
